0,Mnong language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mnong_language,"January 1, 2026 - The Mnong language (also known ... to the Austro-Asiatic language family. It is spoken by the different groups of Mnong in Vietnam and a Pnong group in Cambodia. In Vietnam, Mnong is spoken in the districts of Đăk Song, Đăk Mil, Đăk R'Lấp, Krông Nô, Gia Nghĩa, and other nearby locations in Đắk Nông Province (Nguyễn & Trương 2009). According to Ethnologue, four major dialects exist: Central, Eastern and Southern ...","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
1,Music of Southeast Asia - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Southeast_Asia,"July 9, 2025 - Southeast Asian music encapsulates numerous musical traditions and styles in many countries of Southeast Asia. This subregion consists of eleven countries, namely, Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Timor-Leste and Vietnam, which accommodate ...","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
2,Music of Asia - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Asia,"May 23, 2026 - Asian music encompasses numerous musical styles, diverse cultural and religious traditions, and forms originating on the Asian continent. ... Wikimedia Commons has media related to Asian music.","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
3,Music of Central Asia - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Central_Asia,"May 14, 2026 - The musical traditions of Central Asia mirror the immense diversity found in the cultures and populations residing in the region. Principal instrument types are two- or three-stringed lutes, the necks either fretted or fretless; fiddles made of horsehair; flutes, mostly sige at both ends and ...","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
4,Eastern Mnong - mng (Austro-Asiatic) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/mng/,"October 31, 2025 - Eastern Mnong – mng is in the Austro-Asiatic family, included in Austro-Asiatic, Mon-Khmer, Eastern Mon-Khmer, Bahnaric, South Bahnaric, Sre-Mnong, Mnong, […]","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
5,"Mnong, Eastern in Vietnam people group profile | Joshua Project",https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/19311/VM,"Family relationships are very important in Eastern Mnong society. In some communities, family identity is traced through the mother's line, and women play a strong role in household decisions and property matters. Elders are respected and often guide important community discussions. Community celebrations bring the village together. Festivals connected to the planting season, harvest, or other important events include music, dancing, and shared meals.","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
6,Austro-Asiatic | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/subgroup/231/,"Mnong 4 · Eastern Mnong 1 · Mnong, Eastern mng, a language of Viet Nam · Southern-Central Mnong 3 · Kraol rka, a language of Cambodia · Mnong, Central cmo, a language of Viet Nam · Mnong, Southern mnn, a language of Viet Nam · Sre 2 · Koho kpm, a language of Viet Nam ·","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
7,"Mnong, Eastern Language (MNG) – L1 & L2 Speakers, Status, Map, Endangered Level & Official Use | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/mng/,"Eastern Mnong is a stable indigenous language of Vietnam. It belongs to the Austro-Asiatic language family. Direct evidence is lacking, but the language is thought to be used as a first language by all in the ethnic community. It is not known to be taught in schools.","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
8,Austro-Asiatic Language Family,http://www.ling.fju.edu.tw/typology/Austro-Asiatic.htm,"The Austro-Asiatic family includes 168 languages. Ethnologue subdivides it into two branches: Mon-Khmer with 147 languages, and Munda with 21 languages. Below is a listing of the various groups, their membership size, and areas where they are spoken: · Over two-thirds of these languages are ...","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
9,"Southeast Asian arts - Music, Instruments, Traditions | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/art/Southeast-Asian-arts/Music,"July 26, 1999 - Southeast Asian arts - Music, Instruments, Traditions: A general musical division exists between the urban and rural areas of Southeast Asia. Urban centres comprise the islands of Java and Bali and places in Thailand, Laos, Cambodia, and Myanmar, where big ensembles of gong families play for ...","Mnong, Eastern",Austro-Asiatic
